Before starting a battle,
player [? constructs ?]

00:13:55.070 --> 00:13:59.750
a deck which is composed
of 16 characters among more

00:13:59.750 --> 00:14:02.400
than 3,000 options.

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:04.880
And each character
has its own skills,

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:08.480
and the players have to choose
consistent characters according

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:11.160
to their play styles.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:14.580
As you can imagine, the number
of combinations is extreme,

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:17.250
and it's not easy for
beginners to learn

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:20.640
how to build an optimal deck.

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:23.700
Another issue here is that
building an optimal deck

00:14:23.700 --> 00:14:27.600
requires to learn effective
strategies through the process

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:28.980
of trial and error.

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:34.850
So let me explain why
this is a problem.

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:38.210
Generally speaking, if the
game has complex features which

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:41.120
can distract players
from continuing the game,

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:45.430
the churn rate increases, and
impacts the future revenue.

00:14:45.430 --> 00:14:46.670
Right?

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:48.470
For example, there
is an estimation

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:52.130
that just a few points decrease
in the 30-day churn rate

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:58.400
can increase the revenue by
20% to 30% in the future.

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:00.080
Back to "Othellonia,"
we actually

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:03.980
have a high churn rate,
so we are in trouble.

00:15:03.980 --> 00:15:07.480
This is partly because the
game is complex, and lacks

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:09.960
sufficient [INAUDIBLE] support.

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:11.810
Therefore, it's
hard for beginners

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:14.750
to construct an optimal deck.

00:15:14.750 --> 00:15:16.580
Also, there is the
problem that they

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:19.520
don't have a good
place for practice

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:23.180
in order to improve
their battle skills.

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:27.560
For instance, we only have two
battle options for beginners.

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:31.670
One is NPC battle, in which
they battle against very, very

00:15:31.670 --> 00:15:35.960
weak opponents, and the
other is PvP battle,

00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:41.310
in which they are frequently
matched with skilled players.

00:15:41.310 --> 00:15:45.230
So we needed an opponent
whose play level is similar

00:15:45.230 --> 00:15:46.700
to the beginner's.

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:49.740
So that's our opponent setting.

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:53.030
So let's see our approach.

00:15:53.030 --> 00:15:57.540
To support beginners, we
have developed two functions.

00:15:57.540 --> 00:16:00.440
The first one is the deck
recommendation system,

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:03.620
which selects appropriate
characters from players' piece

00:16:03.620 --> 00:16:08.340
box, and we are using the
association analysis to provide

00:16:08.340 --> 00:16:10.520
a better recommendation.

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:15.450
The second one is Battle AI,
which we call Othellonia Dojo.

00:16:15.450 --> 00:16:20.620
In this function, we developed
AI that can play at any levels.

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:24.730
A deep neural network
is used for this AI.

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:27.280
Later, Ikki will explain
how these techniques

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:28.600
are used in our cases.

00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:36.340
So, by the way, from the
machine learning perspective,

00:16:36.340 --> 00:16:39.860
we need to prepare the
data to build such AIs.

00:16:39.860 --> 00:16:42.450
We are collecting various
kind of in-game data

00:16:42.450 --> 00:16:46.380
since the release of the game,
such as deck logs and battle

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:47.430
logs.

00:16:47.430 --> 00:16:53.330
So we have a good amount of data
before starting this project.

00:16:53.330 --> 00:16:55.930
So in our cases,
we used the data

00:16:55.930 --> 00:16:59.320
for the last nine
months, which contains

00:16:59.320 --> 00:17:01.220
billions of log entries.

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:04.450
And then we filtered the
data by player skills,

00:17:04.450 --> 00:17:09.130
and augmented the original
data in the battle AI cases.

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:12.110
The image on the right
side is an example

00:17:12.110 --> 00:17:16.450
of a battle log, which
is formatted in JSON,

00:17:16.450 --> 00:17:18.609
and describes enter
states and action

00:17:18.609 --> 00:17:21.270
information of the battle.

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:24.390
The bottom diagram shows
a rough data pipeline,

00:17:24.390 --> 00:17:29.760
and BigQuery did a very good job
at this pre-processing phase.

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:34.260
For instance, it can handle
even a terabyte order of data

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:37.590
very fast, and we could
reduce the processing time

00:17:37.590 --> 00:17:39.070
drastically.

00:17:39.070 --> 00:17:44.940
BQ could handle our data in a
few minutes, which took an hour

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:47.980
on on-prem Hadoop clusters.

00:17:47.980 --> 00:17:51.840
And we also love that BQ is easy
to collaborate with other GCP

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:53.450
services.

00:17:53.450 --> 00:17:58.400
So here I want to stress that
our AI has been successfully

00:17:58.400 --> 00:18:02.300
built by sufficient amount of
data and a reliable pipeline.

00:18:06.180 --> 00:18:10.010
So let's see how each
of the cases works.

00:18:10.010 --> 00:18:14.730
This is a very rough overview of
the deck recommendation system.

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:17.630
So we first exploit
a pre-process deck

00:18:17.630 --> 00:18:21.830
log to extract information
about how each characters are

00:18:21.830 --> 00:18:26.170
associated, which is
called associational rules.

00:18:26.170 --> 00:18:28.990
With this analysis, we
can score the strength

00:18:28.990 --> 00:18:34.400
of the relationship across
all the pairs of characters.

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:37.610
Then, if there is a
request from the player,

00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:39.260
the recommendation
server receives

00:18:39.260 --> 00:18:42.080
a set of characters in
the player's piece box,

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:44.990
and it fills all of
the 16 characters

00:18:44.990 --> 00:18:49.880
according to their score
of the associational rules.

00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:52.190
This function is very
flexible, and the player

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:55.610
can specify deck
archetype, deck cost,

00:18:55.610 --> 00:18:59.860
and their favorite characters
to be included in the deck.

00:18:59.860 --> 00:19:03.470
Regarding KPIs, we are
monitoring several metrics.

00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:06.550
For instance, we are
watching how many players

00:19:06.550 --> 00:19:08.930
have used the
recommendation system,

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:12.520
whether the recommended
deck has been accepted,

00:19:12.520 --> 00:19:15.752
and whether the deck contributed
to the players in terms

00:19:15.752 --> 00:19:16.960
of their win rate in battles.

00:19:20.140 --> 00:19:24.160
So as for the Dojo, players
can battle against AI

00:19:24.160 --> 00:19:27.400
with various archetypes
and strengths.

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:29.840
Here we train a
deep neural network

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:32.740
from preprocessed battle logs.

00:19:32.740 --> 00:19:35.320
The clients send
current game status,

00:19:35.320 --> 00:19:37.210
and the battle
API server returns

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:42.350
the inference of neural network
at every turn of the game.

00:19:42.350 --> 00:19:44.730
As for the metrics,
we are monitoring

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:48.630
how frequently the Dojo is
used, and whether the strength

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:53.700
of the AI was sufficient
in terms of win rate.

00:19:53.700 --> 00:19:56.660
So these two AIs have
already been released,

00:19:56.660 --> 00:20:00.410
and we are observing
very positive KPIs.

00:20:00.410 --> 00:20:05.210
So before showing the results,
I'll pass the talk to Ikki.

00:20:05.210 --> 00:20:08.270
He'll talk about the technical
details, and some tips

00:20:08.270 --> 00:20:11.027
for building AI systems.

00:20:11.027 --> 00:20:14.021
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:15.020 --> 00:20:15.980
IKKI: Thank you, Jun.

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:17.890
So hello.

00:20:17.890 --> 00:20:20.370
I'm Ikki, and a data
scientist at DeNA.

00:20:20.370 --> 00:20:24.170
So I am one of the developers
that created our gaming AI

00:20:24.170 --> 00:20:28.460
system by using data analysis,
data science, and machine

00:20:28.460 --> 00:20:29.520
learning.

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:33.050
So today, I'll talk about the
technical details of our use

00:20:33.050 --> 00:20:35.510
cases with GCP.

00:20:35.510 --> 00:20:39.470
So now, let's look at the
story of the technical part.

00:20:39.470 --> 00:20:41.600
So first, I'd like to
standardize our deck

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:42.744
recommendation.

00:20:45.470 --> 00:20:48.950
This is the outline of the
deck recommend algorithm.

00:20:48.950 --> 00:20:52.840
And firstly, the deck
recommendation algorithm

00:20:52.840 --> 00:20:58.720
receives some input from a
player, such as deck archetype,

00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:02.250
deck cost, their possessed
characters, and the characters

00:21:02.250 --> 00:21:05.460
the player wants to use.

00:21:05.460 --> 00:21:08.400
Next, the algorithm
selects a leader

00:21:08.400 --> 00:21:10.620
by using the result
of association

00:21:10.620 --> 00:21:13.570
analysis and statistical data.

00:21:13.570 --> 00:21:17.730
So in this algorithm, the leader
character is chosen first,

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:21.920
and the next characters are
selected based on the leader.

00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:24.740
The well-associated
characters are simply

00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:30.340
selected based on the result
of association analysis.

00:21:30.340 --> 00:21:34.450
If the deck is not full
of the 16 characters,

00:21:34.450 --> 00:21:38.760
another character is selected
as the best character.

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:41.890
After repeating this
procedure several times,

00:21:41.890 --> 00:21:45.820
the recommended deck is
proposed to the player.

00:21:45.820 --> 00:21:48.700
If the game is different,
the way of fine-tuning

00:21:48.700 --> 00:21:51.000
is also different.

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:53.870
So in our game,
"Gyakuten Othellonia,"

00:21:53.870 --> 00:21:57.600
for example, we made additional
rules to decide a leader,

00:21:57.600 --> 00:22:00.920
and we checked its
activation with the skills

00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:01.670
in deck building.

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:08.230
I'd like to give a
brief explanation

00:22:08.230 --> 00:22:10.840
about how to extract
the relationships

00:22:10.840 --> 00:22:12.950
between characters.

00:22:12.950 --> 00:22:16.550
In our recommendation, we are
using association analysis.

00:22:16.550 --> 00:22:19.330
The association analysis
is a rule-based machine

00:22:19.330 --> 00:22:23.390
learning method for discovering
interesting relations

00:22:23.390 --> 00:22:26.390
between contents
in large datasets.

00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:30.500
So this method can perform
high-speed calculation

00:22:30.500 --> 00:22:33.290
to extract the
main relationships,

00:22:33.290 --> 00:22:38.180
and extracted relations are
easy to understand and very

00:22:38.180 --> 00:22:40.740
useful in various settings.

00:22:40.740 --> 00:22:44.480
The relations are evaluated
with some metrics,

00:22:44.480 --> 00:22:48.690
like the strength of the
connections and frequency

00:22:48.690 --> 00:22:51.740
of the rules or patterns.

00:22:51.740 --> 00:22:55.160
Our recommendation builds a
deck by selecting a character

00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:58.040
sequentially with strong
relationships, one

00:22:58.040 --> 00:22:59.680
after another.

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:04.130
So this method can handle
various kinds of data easily.

00:23:04.130 --> 00:23:09.250
So I think this technique
is applicable to many games.

00:23:13.150 --> 00:23:15.460
Next, I will talk
about the system.

00:23:15.460 --> 00:23:19.510
So we built our application
such as deck recommendation

00:23:19.510 --> 00:23:23.200
and the deep learning
AI bot with GCP.

00:23:23.200 --> 00:23:29.270
So why did we choose
GCP for our ML systems?

00:23:29.270 --> 00:23:31.860
There are two main reasons.

00:23:31.860 --> 00:23:34.700
The first one is powerful
and flexible tools

00:23:34.700 --> 00:23:38.330
for machine learning
and data science.

00:23:38.330 --> 00:23:42.480
In the data analysis, we often
try to investigate the data,

00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:47.360
visualize it, and create
the prototype models.

00:23:47.360 --> 00:23:51.230
GCP services for
big data analysis

00:23:51.230 --> 00:23:55.700
make it possible for us, such
as BigQuery, Cloud Datalab,

00:23:55.700 --> 00:23:59.380
and Google Compute Engine.

00:23:59.380 --> 00:24:03.920
The GCP services
for cloud storage

00:24:03.920 --> 00:24:08.040
provides scalable and faster
services for large data.

00:24:08.040 --> 00:24:12.140
And it helps us to manage the
big data and a lot of machine

00:24:12.140 --> 00:24:14.080
learning models.

00:24:14.080 --> 00:24:18.630
About Cloud ML engine, we
can deploy the AI models

00:24:18.630 --> 00:24:21.910
to Cloud ML engine
easily and quickly.

00:24:21.910 --> 00:24:27.320
And it also provides
user-friendly prediction APIs.

00:24:27.320 --> 00:24:31.630
Additionally, GCP provides many
services to perform machine

00:24:31.630 --> 00:24:33.350
learning efficiently.

00:24:33.350 --> 00:24:39.140
For example, we are planning to
use the GKE content of service

00:24:39.140 --> 00:24:43.130
and Cloud ML Engine training
with custom functions

00:24:43.130 --> 00:24:47.100
for scalable and
distributed ML training.

00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:51.950
The second one is scalable and
robust infrastructure services.

00:24:51.950 --> 00:24:53.880
We can run the
application easily

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:57.980
by using Google App Engine with
several programming languages,

00:24:57.980 --> 00:25:01.110
like the Python Go language.

00:25:01.110 --> 00:25:05.340
And we can check many metrics
of system performances

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:09.830
through stack Stackdriver,
Logging, and so on.

00:25:09.830 --> 00:25:12.860
I'd like to emphasize the
auto-scaling function of GAE

00:25:12.860 --> 00:25:17.300
is very great service, because
new instances are increased

00:25:17.300 --> 00:25:20.670
or stopped in
response to traffic.

00:25:20.670 --> 00:25:25.660
And the [INAUDIBLE] are
distributed to the instances

00:25:25.660 --> 00:25:27.590
automatically.

00:25:27.590 --> 00:25:31.100
So this GCP service
is easy to integrate

00:25:31.100 --> 00:25:33.640
into the same systems.

00:25:33.640 --> 00:25:37.640
So for these reasons, we are
choosing GCP for our ML system.

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:44.350
I'll show the system
architecture of deck

00:25:44.350 --> 00:25:46.540
recommendation with GCP.

00:25:46.540 --> 00:25:50.620
First, the deck logs are
saved in BigQuery, which

00:25:50.620 --> 00:25:53.440
are used to extract the rules.

00:25:53.440 --> 00:25:56.140
We send the [INAUDIBLE] schedule
to performance the batch

00:25:56.140 --> 00:25:59.810
processing of the association
analysis in Google Compute

00:25:59.810 --> 00:26:01.180
Engine.

00:26:01.180 --> 00:26:03.640
And in order to catch
up the trained decks

00:26:03.640 --> 00:26:06.910
with latest characters, we are
performing the batch processing

00:26:06.910 --> 00:26:08.722
every day.

00:26:08.722 --> 00:26:13.460
Then Cloud Functions
preprocesses the result

00:26:13.460 --> 00:26:16.130
of association analysis,
and updates the data

00:26:16.130 --> 00:26:18.880
for production environment.

00:26:18.880 --> 00:26:23.510
Cloud Functions checks if the
data is updated correctly--

00:26:23.510 --> 00:26:27.350
for example, if the deck
size is too small or large.

00:26:27.350 --> 00:26:31.580
And we are using Google
App Engine Python

00:26:31.580 --> 00:26:35.110
3.7 for the
recommendation API server.

00:26:35.110 --> 00:26:38.670
The server receives a
request from a client,

00:26:38.670 --> 00:26:41.640
then builds the
deck, and finally, it

00:26:41.640 --> 00:26:45.750
returns the recommended
deck to the player.

00:26:45.750 --> 00:26:48.720
One of the noteworthy
point is GAE Python

00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:52.860
3.7 was generally available
at the end of last year,

00:26:52.860 --> 00:26:57.420
so we could use it
just at the right time.

00:26:57.420 --> 00:27:01.230
As a result of that
[? wait ?] to Python 3.7,

00:27:01.230 --> 00:27:04.740
we don't need to change our
code that we had already

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:06.030
developed in Python 3.

00:27:11.080 --> 00:27:13.780
So far, I've explained the
algorithm and the system

00:27:13.780 --> 00:27:16.020
of deck recommendation.

00:27:16.020 --> 00:27:17.740
Let's move on to
the next topic--

00:27:17.740 --> 00:27:19.280
Deep-Learning AI-Bot.

00:27:19.280 --> 00:27:21.470
It's Othellonia Dojo.

00:27:21.470 --> 00:27:24.990
"Dojo" means a training room to
improve their playing skills.

00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:33.900
This is an overview of the
training in Deep Learning AI

00:27:33.900 --> 00:27:35.040
Bot.

00:27:35.040 --> 00:27:37.070
The branding is
suitable for our case

00:27:37.070 --> 00:27:41.320
to learn the many complicated
features, such as state

00:27:41.320 --> 00:27:45.490
of board, decks, hands,
skills, and combinations

00:27:45.490 --> 00:27:47.650
of the features.

00:27:47.650 --> 00:27:49.720
The Deep Learning AI
Bot receives a state

00:27:49.720 --> 00:27:52.740
of battles from a client.

00:27:52.740 --> 00:27:54.670
The information of
battle is converted

00:27:54.670 --> 00:27:56.800
to the numerical
features, which are

00:27:56.800 --> 00:28:00.310
used as the inputs
of the Deep Learning.

00:28:00.310 --> 00:28:03.660
The network learns the
actions of top-tier players

00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:07.240
as binary classification.

00:28:07.240 --> 00:28:09.170
And finally, the
Deep Learning model

00:28:09.170 --> 00:28:12.250
outputs the values of
each action that will be

00:28:12.250 --> 00:28:17.390
used to decide the next action.

00:28:17.390 --> 00:28:20.623
Here we used more than
10 million battle records

00:28:20.623 --> 00:28:21.290
in the training.

00:28:25.830 --> 00:28:28.830
Next, I will show
the system of AI Bot.

00:28:28.830 --> 00:28:32.710
First of all, I'll talk
about the training phase.

00:28:32.710 --> 00:28:35.030
A large number of
battle records in PvP

00:28:35.030 --> 00:28:40.390
is saved in Google Cloud
Storage through BigQuery.

00:28:40.390 --> 00:28:42.640
And the training
process consists

00:28:42.640 --> 00:28:48.140
of preprocessing of the
features and the model training,

00:28:48.140 --> 00:28:51.960
which are running in
Google Compute Engine.

00:28:51.960 --> 00:28:54.160
At the preprocessing,
the features

00:28:54.160 --> 00:28:59.550
are generated on demand
from the battle records

00:28:59.550 --> 00:29:02.450
by emulating the game.

00:29:02.450 --> 00:29:04.570
In our case, we created
the custom function

00:29:04.570 --> 00:29:09.640
of Data Generator that creates
a feature during the training.

00:29:09.640 --> 00:29:13.210
After the training,
the trained AI model

00:29:13.210 --> 00:29:16.800
is saved in Cloud Storage
to deploy onto the Cloud ML

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:17.300
Engine.

00:29:21.880 --> 00:29:24.730
Next, I move on to
the inference phase.

00:29:24.730 --> 00:29:27.480
So please take a look
at the communication

00:29:27.480 --> 00:29:30.180
between the client
inference API server

00:29:30.180 --> 00:29:32.840
on the left of the slide.

00:29:32.840 --> 00:29:36.440
The battle records are posted
to the inference API server

00:29:36.440 --> 00:29:41.261
from the client, which plays
a battle event against AI bot.

00:29:41.261 --> 00:29:44.260
The inference API server
reproduces a battle

00:29:44.260 --> 00:29:47.500
from the record,
extracts the features,

00:29:47.500 --> 00:29:50.620
and it sends those features
to the online prediction

00:29:50.620 --> 00:29:54.010
API with Cloud ML Engine.

00:29:54.010 --> 00:29:55.970
Online prediction
of Cloud ML Engine

00:29:55.970 --> 00:29:59.600
is very useful,
because it is optimized

00:29:59.600 --> 00:30:05.110
to run the AI models with
low [INAUDIBLE] in real time.

00:30:05.110 --> 00:30:09.950
Cloud ML Engine uses the
trained AI model starting in DCS

00:30:09.950 --> 00:30:13.860
and returns the
values of each action.

00:30:13.860 --> 00:30:16.650
The values are sent
back to the client,

00:30:16.650 --> 00:30:19.860
and the client moves
towards the battle step

00:30:19.860 --> 00:30:25.420
by selecting a best action
according to the values.

00:30:25.420 --> 00:30:27.690
That's the overall
communication between the client

00:30:27.690 --> 00:30:28.357
and the servers.

00:30:30.760 --> 00:30:33.190
Google App Engine
and Cloud ML Engine

00:30:33.190 --> 00:30:37.300
can auto-scale in response
to the load on the server.

00:30:37.300 --> 00:30:39.480
That's a very good function.

00:30:39.480 --> 00:30:42.830
So by taking the benefit
of the auto-scaling,

00:30:42.830 --> 00:30:44.775
we can manage a large
amount of requests.

00:30:50.150 --> 00:30:52.290
Lastly, I'll touch on
the technical topic--

00:30:52.290 --> 00:30:55.100
the testing of the systems.

00:30:55.100 --> 00:30:58.500
So why should we
test the systems?

00:30:58.500 --> 00:31:02.550
I mentioned in the last slide,
Cloud ML Engine can auto-scale

00:31:02.550 --> 00:31:03.490
quickly--

00:31:03.490 --> 00:31:04.620
that's good.

00:31:04.620 --> 00:31:06.260
So yes, that's true.

00:31:06.260 --> 00:31:10.930
But we didn't have enough
knowledge about how much load

00:31:10.930 --> 00:31:13.710
the system withstand.

00:31:13.710 --> 00:31:15.540
In the mobile game,
generally, the load

00:31:15.540 --> 00:31:18.970
tends to increase at the
moment a new event opens.

00:31:21.640 --> 00:31:24.480
The figure on the
slide shows an example

00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:29.650
of estimated requests per
second in a yardstick event.

00:31:29.650 --> 00:31:32.036
This is a trial calculation.

00:31:32.036 --> 00:31:35.740
The requests per second will
increase to several hundreds

00:31:35.740 --> 00:31:37.900
in 10 minutes.

00:31:37.900 --> 00:31:41.620
We have to check in
advance if the system can

00:31:41.620 --> 00:31:44.910
withstand this load.

00:31:44.910 --> 00:31:50.250
As a point of notice, we cannot
predict the action of the AI

00:31:50.250 --> 00:31:54.630
as a batch processing
at regular intervals.

00:31:54.630 --> 00:31:57.060
Our system has to
respond to the request

00:31:57.060 --> 00:32:00.330
from the player in real time.

00:32:00.330 --> 00:32:07.950
That is where the AI should put
the character in the next turn.

00:32:07.950 --> 00:32:12.130
To achieve this, we chose
the Online Prediction API

00:32:12.130 --> 00:32:13.200
of the Cloud ML Engine.

00:32:18.580 --> 00:32:21.840
We've checked if the Cloud
ML Engine can satisfy

00:32:21.840 --> 00:32:24.160
our requirements by
performing the load

00:32:24.160 --> 00:32:28.060
testings in advance of release.

00:32:28.060 --> 00:32:30.430
In our case, the
testing was performed

00:32:30.430 --> 00:32:34.240
in the setting and the requests
increase linearly and reach

00:32:34.240 --> 00:32:38.990
a maximum of 1,000
requests per second.

00:32:38.990 --> 00:32:42.920
We found the Cloud ML Engine can
auto-scale quickly in response

00:32:42.920 --> 00:32:47.010
to the requests, and the average
latency of the prediction

00:32:47.010 --> 00:32:50.050
is less than one second.

00:32:50.050 --> 00:32:52.180
However, there are
a few requests that

00:32:52.180 --> 00:32:55.330
took a longer time than usual.

00:32:55.330 --> 00:33:01.850
Since this might not be possible
to request to the player,

00:33:01.850 --> 00:33:05.270
so we needed to
resolve this condition.

00:33:05.270 --> 00:33:08.330
Our DeNA team and
Cloud ML Engine team

00:33:08.330 --> 00:33:12.020
cooperated to investigate
the bottlenecks.

00:33:12.020 --> 00:33:16.540
And then we found the
timeout has happened

00:33:16.540 --> 00:33:21.320
when the new instances of app in
the process of the auto-scaling

00:33:21.320 --> 00:33:23.970
due to our heavy load.

00:33:23.970 --> 00:33:27.240
It is challenging to handle a
large number of requests which

00:33:27.240 --> 00:33:31.310
consists of many features
at the same time with online

00:33:31.310 --> 00:33:35.430
[? prediction. ?] But if
we can resolve this problem

00:33:35.430 --> 00:33:40.770
by building an efficient
but consistent,

00:33:40.770 --> 00:33:43.860
we don't have to warm up
the system beforehand,

00:33:43.860 --> 00:33:47.750
and the system will
be more robust.

00:33:47.750 --> 00:33:50.790
So our team took our time
to deal with this problem.

00:33:54.710 --> 00:34:01.580
Let's take a look at we
tackled to remove the errors.

00:34:01.580 --> 00:34:08.389
When the error
returns within a time

00:34:08.389 --> 00:34:11.980
we can simply throw it
back again to the servers.

00:34:11.980 --> 00:34:15.880
But in the case of a timeout,
the system cannot send it back

00:34:15.880 --> 00:34:20.290
again, because a timeout
error is not returned.

00:34:20.290 --> 00:34:23.199
Therefore, when the
inference API server does not

00:34:23.199 --> 00:34:27.370
receive the response
within five seconds,

00:34:27.370 --> 00:34:29.469
the request is sent
back to the API

00:34:29.469 --> 00:34:35.219
again, because something bad
has happened in the inference.

00:34:35.219 --> 00:34:39.690
Also, if the destination of the
request is the same end point,

00:34:39.690 --> 00:34:43.250
it will further increase
the load on the server.

00:34:43.250 --> 00:34:46.429
As a result, we set on
the backup end point

00:34:46.429 --> 00:34:49.190
to receive the request
that fails to be

00:34:49.190 --> 00:34:51.820
operated in the main endpoint.

00:34:51.820 --> 00:34:54.850
That is, the second
request is sent

00:34:54.850 --> 00:34:58.360
to the backup endpoint, which
is prepared independently

00:34:58.360 --> 00:34:59.350
from the main endpoint.

00:35:03.480 --> 00:35:07.500
This is the result of load
testing with backup endpoint.

00:35:07.500 --> 00:35:10.590
The figure shows the error
rate of several settings--

00:35:10.590 --> 00:35:13.500
no backup, backup
with 10 minimum nodes,

00:35:13.500 --> 00:35:17.010
and backup with
20 minimum nodes.

00:35:17.010 --> 00:35:20.610
In the case without backup
endpoint, on the left,

00:35:20.610 --> 00:35:24.650
about 1.3% of errors remain.

00:35:24.650 --> 00:35:28.130
In the middle setting of the
backup with 10 minimum nodes,

00:35:28.130 --> 00:35:30.320
although small errors
still remained,

00:35:30.320 --> 00:35:36.190
the backup can deal with most
of the main endpoint errors.

00:35:36.190 --> 00:35:38.410
We found the reason
of these small errors

00:35:38.410 --> 00:35:44.000
is because the number of backup
instances was small a bit.

00:35:44.000 --> 00:35:47.830
So the timeout
has still happened

00:35:47.830 --> 00:35:50.140
at the warm-up of
the auto-scaling

00:35:50.140 --> 00:35:52.530
in the backup environment.

00:35:52.530 --> 00:35:55.230
So right most cases
of fine tune setting,

00:35:55.230 --> 00:35:56.730
where the number
of backup instances

00:35:56.730 --> 00:36:01.580
was set to 20 for covering
the errors of main endpoint

00:36:01.580 --> 00:36:03.560
substantially.

00:36:03.560 --> 00:36:05.810
In this case, we could
handle all the requests

00:36:05.810 --> 00:36:08.330
without errors.

00:36:08.330 --> 00:36:11.920
So in this way, DeNA and
the Cloud ML Engine team

00:36:11.920 --> 00:36:15.940
got over the challenge of high
and spiky traffic and latency

00:36:15.940 --> 00:36:18.910
limit without errors.

00:36:18.910 --> 00:36:21.670
After deployment,
this system has been

00:36:21.670 --> 00:36:25.580
stable in handling many errors.

00:36:25.580 --> 00:36:30.980
So far, I've explained the
[INAUDIBLE] system of our AI,

00:36:30.980 --> 00:36:35.630
and the system with GCP
can be constructed flexibly

00:36:35.630 --> 00:36:39.200
to cope with various
programs for gaming.

00:36:39.200 --> 00:36:42.610
So now I'd like to hand
over to Jun for the KPI

00:36:42.610 --> 00:36:44.430
result and the future.

00:36:44.430 --> 00:36:45.746
Thanks.

00:36:45.746 --> 00:36:49.127
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:49.820 --> 00:36:50.320
JUN: Yeah.

00:36:50.320 --> 00:36:51.580
Thank you, Ikki.

00:36:51.580 --> 00:36:56.140
And I think you mentioned, we
needed a scalable and robust

00:36:56.140 --> 00:37:01.360
system to actually implement
this AI in the real cases.

00:37:01.360 --> 00:37:03.220
So in this final
part, I would like

00:37:03.220 --> 00:37:05.420
to share what happened
after the release.

00:37:08.460 --> 00:37:11.640
So first, for the
deck recommendation,

00:37:11.640 --> 00:37:14.760
here is a chart which shows
the number of beginners

00:37:14.760 --> 00:37:17.050
who use deck recommendation.

00:37:17.050 --> 00:37:21.350
There were constant usage after
the release, but at this time,

00:37:21.350 --> 00:37:24.610
this function was
not well-known.

00:37:27.550 --> 00:37:29.850
But the station has
been changed after we

00:37:29.850 --> 00:37:32.520
started an in-game
event to promote

00:37:32.520 --> 00:37:35.160
players to build new decks.

00:37:35.160 --> 00:37:38.550
The deck recommendation system
has been recognized more,

00:37:38.550 --> 00:37:41.880
and even after the
event has been finished,

00:37:41.880 --> 00:37:47.450
many players are still using
this function frequently.

00:37:47.450 --> 00:37:53.270
So second, we are very glad
that the recommended deck pushed

00:37:53.270 --> 00:37:56.420
the win rate of the
beginners by about 5%

00:37:56.420 --> 00:37:59.510
compared to decks created
without the recommendation

00:37:59.510 --> 00:38:01.010
system.

00:38:01.010 --> 00:38:03.950
Since our focus was
to support beginners,

00:38:03.950 --> 00:38:07.580
we are very satisfied
with these results.

00:38:07.580 --> 00:38:11.210
Also, I'd like to mention that
this function has good adoption

00:38:11.210 --> 00:38:14.620
even for top-tier players.

00:38:14.620 --> 00:38:17.920
This was sort of
byproduct, but we're

00:38:17.920 --> 00:38:20.080
observing that heavy
players usually

00:38:20.080 --> 00:38:22.310
update their deck frequently.

00:38:22.310 --> 00:38:25.030
And this recommendation
system mitigates the cost

00:38:25.030 --> 00:38:27.850
of building deck from scratch.

00:38:27.850 --> 00:38:30.540
They use recommendation
as a first edition,

00:38:30.540 --> 00:38:32.577
and then customize
the deck as they want.

00:38:35.990 --> 00:38:39.770
So let's look at the
system metrics, as well.

00:38:39.770 --> 00:38:42.800
After the release, we
have a stable latency

00:38:42.800 --> 00:38:49.040
of 20 milliseconds, which is
very fast enough for our cases.

00:38:49.040 --> 00:38:51.410
But we are very
surprised by the result,

00:38:51.410 --> 00:38:55.010
because the recommendation
algorithm is very complex,

00:38:55.010 --> 00:38:58.320
and we haven't expected
such a low latency.

00:38:58.320 --> 00:39:01.450
The auto-scaling does
a good job, as well,

00:39:01.450 --> 00:39:04.130
and we haven't had any
system trouble so far.

00:39:06.850 --> 00:39:09.610
So briefly, we will explain
about the evaluation

00:39:09.610 --> 00:39:12.430
of Deep Learning AI Bot.

00:39:12.430 --> 00:39:14.410
After the release,
we are observing

00:39:14.410 --> 00:39:18.940
that our AI Bot has good
enough win rate as a practice

00:39:18.940 --> 00:39:21.370
partner for beginners.

00:39:21.370 --> 00:39:24.520
Our AI Bot can beat
even top-tier players,

00:39:24.520 --> 00:39:28.550
and therefore, the players
can choose any levels of AI

00:39:28.550 --> 00:39:31.720
as a sort of mentor.

00:39:31.720 --> 00:39:33.520
Although this was
released last week,

00:39:33.520 --> 00:39:37.720
and we don't have enough data
to evaluate the business impact,

00:39:37.720 --> 00:39:40.660
Dojo is used by
beginner very well,

00:39:40.660 --> 00:39:45.690
and we hope this can lead
good KPI in the future.

00:39:45.690 --> 00:39:48.720
So as to the latency, the
inference of Machine Learning

00:39:48.720 --> 00:39:53.270
Engine is about 200
to 300 milliseconds,

00:39:53.270 --> 00:39:55.910
and they're very
fast and stable.

00:39:55.910 --> 00:39:58.640
Even for the entire
system, the response time

00:39:58.640 --> 00:40:03.230
is about a second, which is
enough fast for our case.

00:40:03.230 --> 00:40:06.080
Backup endpoint is working
well, and we haven't

00:40:06.080 --> 00:40:07.880
had any system trouble so far.

00:40:10.750 --> 00:40:13.960
So to the end of my
talk, I'll show items

00:40:13.960 --> 00:40:17.170
which worked well in our cases.

00:40:17.170 --> 00:40:21.760
So to train the AI that
played the game like a human,

00:40:21.760 --> 00:40:25.640
we needed a battle log
that completely reproduced

00:40:25.640 --> 00:40:28.340
battle status and actions.

00:40:28.340 --> 00:40:31.820
So if you have some random
effects in the game,

00:40:31.820 --> 00:40:36.930
make sure that the random
seeds are stored in the logs.

00:40:36.930 --> 00:40:39.930
Secondly, for the
evolution of our AI,

00:40:39.930 --> 00:40:42.240
we needed to run
thousands of battles

00:40:42.240 --> 00:40:45.840
to get performance statistics.

00:40:45.840 --> 00:40:49.810
Headless simulator helped
us a lot in this context.

00:40:49.810 --> 00:40:52.600
If you want to try to use
reinforcement learning

00:40:52.600 --> 00:40:55.390
techniques, you also
need very fast simulator,

00:40:55.390 --> 00:40:58.690
because the number of
battles that our version goes

00:40:58.690 --> 00:41:01.570
through really matters.

00:41:01.570 --> 00:41:04.040
And third, we also
want to stress

00:41:04.040 --> 00:41:08.120
that scalable and robust system
is required for implementing

00:41:08.120 --> 00:41:10.680
AI in real services.

00:41:10.680 --> 00:41:12.690
We think that GCP--

00:41:12.690 --> 00:41:14.970
in particular, Machine
Learning Engine and GAE--

00:41:14.970 --> 00:41:17.980
were a good choice for us.

00:41:17.980 --> 00:41:22.180
The last item is not
technical, but critical.

00:41:22.180 --> 00:41:26.230
Generally speaking, we don't
know how well AI will perform

00:41:26.230 --> 00:41:28.210
and how big the
business impact will

00:41:28.210 --> 00:41:30.910
be before starting a project.

00:41:30.910 --> 00:41:33.790
So in such an
uncertain situation,

00:41:33.790 --> 00:41:35.440
the communication
and controlling

00:41:35.440 --> 00:41:38.730
the expectation
is very important.

00:41:38.730 --> 00:41:41.310
Although we are machine
learning engineers,

00:41:41.310 --> 00:41:44.850
we try to understand the
game and the business issue

00:41:44.850 --> 00:41:46.030
correctly.

00:41:46.030 --> 00:41:49.650
And I think that this was
a key factor of the success

00:41:49.650 --> 00:41:52.070
of these projects.

00:41:52.070 --> 00:41:56.140
So this is from me, and I'd like
to pass the summary to Samir

00:41:56.140 --> 00:41:57.070
again.

00:41:57.070 --> 00:41:58.662
Thanks.

00:41:58.662 --> 00:42:00.400
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:00.400 --> 00:42:02.340
SAMIR: Yes.

00:42:02.340 --> 00:42:06.580
Thank you, Jun-san and Ikki-san,
