00:42:06.580 --> 00:42:09.840
I think it's inspired a
lot of people here today,

00:42:09.840 --> 00:42:12.090
and it's very
impressive, the work

00:42:12.090 --> 00:42:14.460
they have done to make
their game a bit more

00:42:14.460 --> 00:42:18.540
inclusive and a bit more
friendly to beginners.

00:42:18.540 --> 00:42:20.940
And I hope that what
they have implemented

00:42:20.940 --> 00:42:23.910
will be very successful, and
that today's presentation

00:42:23.910 --> 00:42:26.280
can help you, maybe,
have some ideas.

00:42:26.280 --> 00:42:28.980
And so, before
closing this session,

00:42:28.980 --> 00:42:31.830
I want just to show
you some simple use

00:42:31.830 --> 00:42:36.030
case that you could start
with using machine learning.

00:42:36.030 --> 00:42:39.660
And I will talk quickly
about two that don't much

00:42:39.660 --> 00:42:41.910
require any expertise,
and one that's very

00:42:41.910 --> 00:42:44.050
similar to what they have done.

00:42:44.050 --> 00:42:48.900
So starting by this one,
the chat translation.

00:42:48.900 --> 00:42:53.700
So one of the big problem in
games is the language barrier.

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:57.240
And so I strongly see that in
the regions where I come from--

00:42:57.240 --> 00:43:00.900
Japan, where players are
just speaking Japanese.

00:43:00.900 --> 00:43:03.120
And you will never
see a game that

00:43:03.120 --> 00:43:05.760
has multiple community
of people from Japan

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:07.800
playing with Korean
and Taiwanese people,

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:09.670
because the language.

00:43:09.670 --> 00:43:12.270
And so if you want to
break that barrier,

00:43:12.270 --> 00:43:16.020
you can start by using this
kind of pre-trained model,

00:43:16.020 --> 00:43:19.740
like Translation
API, to translate

00:43:19.740 --> 00:43:24.180
chat messages in real time, and
to break those kind of barrier.

00:43:24.180 --> 00:43:29.040
It would be so good to have
more integrated community

00:43:29.040 --> 00:43:32.460
within the game that
could go beyond frontiers.

00:43:32.460 --> 00:43:35.730
So this is one use case
that you could really

00:43:35.730 --> 00:43:37.390
start very quickly.

00:43:37.390 --> 00:43:39.900
And if you have ever
tried the translation--

00:43:39.900 --> 00:43:42.310
Google Translation-- you
see that it's very accurate.

00:43:42.310 --> 00:43:45.840
And even though it might not
be perfect in the language

00:43:45.840 --> 00:43:50.040
of a game, it will still be
better than having communities

00:43:50.040 --> 00:43:53.930
that cannot communicate
with each other.

00:43:53.930 --> 00:43:59.500
The second one is one that I
see every gaming company trying

00:43:59.500 --> 00:44:01.310
to fight, is the
player toxicity.

00:44:01.310 --> 00:44:03.310
Sometimes in your
game, you might

00:44:03.310 --> 00:44:07.360
have some people that might
bully some other player,

00:44:07.360 --> 00:44:10.770
and that can break
a bit the game.

00:44:10.770 --> 00:44:14.450
And so you don't want to lose
important players because

00:44:14.450 --> 00:44:17.390
of the bad players' behavior.

00:44:17.390 --> 00:44:20.750
You could start on Google
Cloud Platform-- on GCP--

00:44:20.750 --> 00:44:22.850
using Natural Language
Processing API.

00:44:22.850 --> 00:44:26.760
That has a functionality
called Sentiment Analysis.

00:44:26.760 --> 00:44:29.540
So what Sentiment
Analysis is doing is,

00:44:29.540 --> 00:44:32.480
it would take a
text as an input,

00:44:32.480 --> 00:44:35.180
and will be able
to tell you if it's

00:44:35.180 --> 00:44:38.630
a positive input or a negative
input that has been done.

00:44:38.630 --> 00:44:42.930
Based on a lot of data that
you would be able to take,

00:44:42.930 --> 00:44:46.640
you could maybe detect very
negative insight from players,

00:44:46.640 --> 00:44:50.330
and to warn them,
maybe to ban them,

00:44:50.330 --> 00:44:52.200
if it's really at that point.

00:44:52.200 --> 00:44:54.320
But you don't want,
maybe, to go manually

00:44:54.320 --> 00:44:57.880
to check every single
chat or action of players

00:44:57.880 --> 00:45:00.770
to decide if that
person is toxic.

00:45:00.770 --> 00:45:03.320
You could get this
kind of API to bring

00:45:03.320 --> 00:45:06.590
you insight on potential
problems in your game,

00:45:06.590 --> 00:45:09.980
and potential players
that are dealing,

00:45:09.980 --> 00:45:11.600
maybe, problems in
your game, and that

00:45:11.600 --> 00:45:13.680
can damage it in the end.

00:45:13.680 --> 00:45:18.020
So these are two use cases
that can be start very quickly,

00:45:18.020 --> 00:45:19.790
using pre-trained models.

00:45:19.790 --> 00:45:23.570
The last one, which will
require a bit more expertise,

00:45:23.570 --> 00:45:27.890
and I think that's very similar
to what DeNA has done today,

00:45:27.890 --> 00:45:29.870
is simplifying games.

00:45:29.870 --> 00:45:32.000
And I think today,
what DeNA have done

00:45:32.000 --> 00:45:35.150
is a perfect example of
what is simplification

00:45:35.150 --> 00:45:36.410
of game using AI.

00:45:36.410 --> 00:45:38.450
And they made their
deck recommendation

00:45:38.450 --> 00:45:40.820
to simplify a bit the
construction of the games

00:45:40.820 --> 00:45:43.620
when you have too many
characters, possibly.

00:45:43.620 --> 00:45:46.775
And also, having a way
to train against a bot

00:45:46.775 --> 00:45:51.230
is also a way to train
and help people ramp up.

00:45:51.230 --> 00:45:53.870
So you could also think
from other use cases.

00:45:53.870 --> 00:45:56.390
And one that I always
hear from studios

00:45:56.390 --> 00:45:59.630
is the Q and A. So
the QA testing--

00:45:59.630 --> 00:46:00.890
not Q and A. QA testing.

00:46:00.890 --> 00:46:03.920
So QA testing costs
a lot of money,

00:46:03.920 --> 00:46:06.770
because you need to test every
time you do some changes.

00:46:06.770 --> 00:46:09.860
You need to test if this
doesn't break, for instance,

00:46:09.860 --> 00:46:11.510
the balance of your game.

00:46:11.510 --> 00:46:15.080
In the mobile space,
and even now in console,

00:46:15.080 --> 00:46:18.530
because you start to see that
the latest game, like Fortnite,

00:46:18.530 --> 00:46:22.700
gets weekly updates to bring
you new items in the game.

00:46:22.700 --> 00:46:26.300
If you will be able to
automate this kind of testing,

00:46:26.300 --> 00:46:28.040
you'll be able to
be sure that when

00:46:28.040 --> 00:46:31.020
you bring a new item or
a new card in your game,

00:46:31.020 --> 00:46:33.230
it doesn't break the
balance of the game.

00:46:33.230 --> 00:46:36.650
And usually, I can see
from being in Japan,

00:46:36.650 --> 00:46:38.600
and playing those
Japanese game on mobile

00:46:38.600 --> 00:46:41.660
that brings new item, new
character every two weeks--

00:46:41.660 --> 00:46:44.590
sometimes a character
will break the balance.

00:46:44.590 --> 00:46:47.060
I even see on Fortnite,
sometimes they

00:46:47.060 --> 00:46:49.310
bring a new weapon,
and it's too strong,

00:46:49.310 --> 00:46:51.320
and they immediately
take action.

00:46:51.320 --> 00:46:53.690
But you would like to
act before doing that,

00:46:53.690 --> 00:46:56.270
before going in production
with your update.

00:46:56.270 --> 00:47:01.080
If you'll be able to create
the automation, like they did,

00:47:01.080 --> 00:47:03.050
that would be one
of the use cases

00:47:03.050 --> 00:47:04.360
that could be interesting.

00:47:04.360 --> 00:47:06.890
And I think to do
this kind of thing,

00:47:06.890 --> 00:47:09.550
you'll be able to use
TensorFlow, Machine Learning

00:47:09.550 --> 00:47:12.770
Engine, and do something very
similar to what DeNA did.

00:47:12.770 --> 00:47:15.860
And I hope that what the
presentation from DeNA

00:47:15.860 --> 00:47:18.020
inspired you and
gave you some insight

00:47:18.020 --> 00:47:23.210
of how you bring and approach
a problem that's as complex as,

00:47:23.210 --> 00:47:25.670
maybe, a human-simulated bot.

00:47:25.670 --> 00:47:27.800
But I hope this will
be helpful for you.

00:47:27.800 --> 00:47:30.770
And I think we have
a little time--

00:47:30.770 --> 00:47:32.720
I think we have 10 minutes more.

00:47:32.720 --> 00:47:35.980
So if you have any
questions, I think we're--

00:47:35.980 --> 00:47:39.270
Ikki-san, Jun-san-- please
come up on the stage.

00:47:39.270 --> 00:47:40.880
And if you have any
questions, I think

00:47:40.880 --> 00:47:42.950
they have a mic in the back.

00:47:42.950 --> 00:47:45.590
And feel free to ask question.

00:47:45.590 --> 00:47:48.352
If you want to talk a bit more
deeper about something, or a

00:47:48.352 --> 00:47:50.060
challenge that you've
been having, we can

00:47:50.060 --> 00:47:51.620
discuss after the session.

00:47:51.620 --> 00:47:52.957
So thank you very much.

00:47:52.957 --> 00:47:56.436
[APPLAUSE]

00:48:00.420 --> 00:48:01.440
Any question?

00:48:01.440 --> 00:48:01.940
Ah.

00:48:05.620 --> 00:48:07.720
AUDIENCE: First, thank
you for your talk.

00:48:07.720 --> 00:48:10.815
When training your AI based
on different skill levels--

00:48:10.815 --> 00:48:12.940
so to play against beginners
versus playing against

00:48:12.940 --> 00:48:14.020
experts--

00:48:14.020 --> 00:48:16.840
did you ask the Cloud
system, the ML system

00:48:16.840 --> 00:48:18.460
for multiple inferences?

00:48:18.460 --> 00:48:20.285
Or did you train it differently?

00:48:20.285 --> 00:48:22.660
Did you train it on games for
people who lose frequently,

00:48:22.660 --> 00:48:25.950
as opposed to just experts?

00:48:25.950 --> 00:48:29.670
JUN: So your question
is that if we only

00:48:29.670 --> 00:48:33.050
train with the top-tier
tier players [INAUDIBLE]??

00:48:33.050 --> 00:48:33.550
Ah.

00:48:33.550 --> 00:48:33.690
Yeah.

00:48:33.690 --> 00:48:34.250
Yeah.

00:48:34.250 --> 00:48:35.070
That's right.

00:48:35.070 --> 00:48:37.195
AUDIENCE: You want to train
it so that it sometimes

00:48:37.195 --> 00:48:38.710
loses to beginners.

00:48:38.710 --> 00:48:40.950
So how did you
accomplish that goal?

00:48:40.950 --> 00:48:43.300
JUN: You mean that--
to train the levels?

00:48:43.300 --> 00:48:43.800
Yeah.

00:48:43.800 --> 00:48:43.980
Yeah.

00:48:43.980 --> 00:48:44.480
OK.

00:48:44.480 --> 00:48:48.950
So we have invented some
schemes for that adjustment.

00:48:51.680 --> 00:48:58.020
So we inference for every
turn the score of the move.

00:48:58.020 --> 00:48:58.640
Right?

00:48:58.640 --> 00:49:01.980
So if, for example,
there is ten moves,

00:49:01.980 --> 00:49:08.700
we sort every latest score, and
we pick the most highest score,

00:49:08.700 --> 00:49:10.200
up to--

00:49:10.200 --> 00:49:11.910
I mean, to be strong.

00:49:11.910 --> 00:49:12.540
Right?

00:49:12.540 --> 00:49:18.960
But we are optimizing
that score by selecting

00:49:18.960 --> 00:49:24.390
the second- and third-best
move in a certain probability,

00:49:24.390 --> 00:49:28.680
and that's a hyper parameter,
and we are tuning manually

00:49:28.680 --> 00:49:29.910
that parameter.

00:49:29.910 --> 00:49:31.240
So that's why we are--

00:49:31.240 --> 00:49:36.650
we could balance the levels
according to the player.

00:49:40.980 --> 00:49:42.740
AUDIENCE: In building
the game bot,

00:49:42.740 --> 00:49:46.010
did you use
reinforcement learning,

00:49:46.010 --> 00:49:50.390
or improvised
learning, or maybe you

00:49:50.390 --> 00:49:52.740
had different results
with different models?

00:49:52.740 --> 00:49:56.265
JUN: So in this case, we are
not using, but we are trying.

00:49:56.265 --> 00:49:57.890
Well, actually, the
[INAUDIBLE],, here,

00:49:57.890 --> 00:50:01.550
is that the [? main ?] function
running is very unstable

00:50:01.550 --> 00:50:02.600
to learn--

00:50:02.600 --> 00:50:07.250
especially in this
kind of strategic game.

00:50:07.250 --> 00:50:10.220
So while we are trying, but
the win rate is not so high,

00:50:10.220 --> 00:50:13.500
like a supervised
learning AI bot.

00:50:19.474 --> 00:50:19.974
SAMIR: Here.

00:50:27.473 --> 00:50:28.140
AUDIENCE: Hello.

00:50:28.140 --> 00:50:29.490
Thanks for your talk again.

00:50:29.490 --> 00:50:34.080
So you had nine months of data,
and billions of logs to start.

00:50:34.080 --> 00:50:36.612
If you were to put this
system into a brand-new game,

00:50:36.612 --> 00:50:38.820
nine months is a very long
time, and a lot of players

00:50:38.820 --> 00:50:40.330
may already come and churn.

00:50:40.330 --> 00:50:45.000
So what would be an
ideal-- what would be sort

00:50:45.000 --> 00:50:46.230
of the theoretical minimum?

00:50:46.230 --> 00:50:48.522
Could you put this in at the
start and learn as you go?

00:50:48.522 --> 00:50:50.758
Would you need to wait some
certain amount of time to

00:50:50.758 --> 00:50:52.010
[? accomplish this? ?]

00:50:52.010 --> 00:50:52.510
JUN: Yeah.

00:50:52.510 --> 00:50:55.020
Actually, like, two
week or one month's data

00:50:55.020 --> 00:50:56.100
is enough for our case.

00:50:56.100 --> 00:50:59.070
So nine months is
a kind of buffer.

00:50:59.070 --> 00:51:02.340
So I think it's too much
to use nine month's data.

00:51:02.340 --> 00:51:05.430
But you are measuring
the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:51:05.430 --> 00:51:07.294
AUDIENCE: If you could--

00:51:07.294 --> 00:51:09.264
moving this to
your next game, you

00:51:09.264 --> 00:51:10.556
would have this at the startup?

00:51:10.556 --> 00:51:11.056
JUN: Ah.

00:51:11.056 --> 00:51:11.560
Yeah.

00:51:11.560 --> 00:51:14.830
So in that case, maybe we
need like one month data,

00:51:14.830 --> 00:51:17.860
or a few weeks' data,
before the training.

00:51:23.330 --> 00:51:25.266
SAMIR: Any other questions?

00:51:25.266 --> 00:51:25.766
Ah.

00:51:25.766 --> 00:51:26.710
Over there.

00:51:30.860 --> 00:51:31.360
Yes.

00:51:31.360 --> 00:51:33.443
By the way, thank you for
the very good questions.

00:51:33.443 --> 00:51:35.503
It's a very good point.

00:51:35.503 --> 00:51:37.420
AUDIENCE: The nature of
the games, these days,

00:51:37.420 --> 00:51:41.350
is that the rules of the games
change frequently-- especially

00:51:41.350 --> 00:51:43.360
with game updates.

00:51:43.360 --> 00:51:46.360
And that changes the nature of
how the bots should be playing.

00:51:46.360 --> 00:51:48.230
How do you plan
to deal with that?

00:51:48.230 --> 00:51:48.730
JUN: Yeah.

00:51:48.730 --> 00:51:50.500
That's a good point.

00:51:50.500 --> 00:51:54.100
And actually, we're releasing
a few characters every week,

00:51:54.100 --> 00:51:57.580
and the game bots
change time by time.

00:51:57.580 --> 00:52:02.750
But our deep learning frameworks
works even for that cases,

00:52:02.750 --> 00:52:03.900
because--

00:52:03.900 --> 00:52:08.950
well, just after using one
week or a few days' data,

00:52:08.950 --> 00:52:12.910
the AI become to add up to--

00:52:12.910 --> 00:52:13.660
how do you say--

00:52:13.660 --> 00:52:16.730
the latest environments.

00:52:16.730 --> 00:52:21.290
So there is kind of a one
week delay, but in this case,

00:52:21.290 --> 00:52:22.870
I think we are acceptable.

00:52:22.870 --> 00:52:26.530
So yeah.

00:52:26.530 --> 00:52:27.900
That's answer for the question.

00:52:36.270 --> 00:52:41.510
AUDIENCE: What other problems
are you trying to tackle?

00:52:41.510 --> 00:52:44.120
JUN: So your question is
what's the problem, currently,

00:52:44.120 --> 00:52:44.620
we have?

00:52:44.620 --> 00:52:45.610
Or--

00:52:45.610 --> 00:52:47.530
AUDIENCE: Is there any
other type of problem

00:52:47.530 --> 00:52:49.420
that you're
considering tackling,

00:52:49.420 --> 00:52:52.100
other than what you
presented today?

00:52:52.100 --> 00:52:54.520
SAMIR: If there is any
other problem that you

00:52:54.520 --> 00:52:56.810
want to solve using ML?

00:52:56.810 --> 00:52:57.310
JUN: Ah.

00:52:57.310 --> 00:52:58.570
Yeah, of course.

00:52:58.570 --> 00:53:02.360
So I mentioned the importance
of machine learning.

00:53:02.360 --> 00:53:05.830
But actually, we want
to keep the game balance

00:53:05.830 --> 00:53:09.310
very stable, and before
releasing the characters.

00:53:09.310 --> 00:53:09.970
Right?

00:53:09.970 --> 00:53:13.900
So we want to implement
reinforcement learning engine

00:53:13.900 --> 00:53:18.100
to learn the brand-new
characters before release,

00:53:18.100 --> 00:53:22.970
and then check if the
balance is not too much--

00:53:22.970 --> 00:53:27.270
I mean, strength, or strong or
weak, or something like that.

00:53:27.270 --> 00:53:30.110
So that might be
the next challenge.

00:53:35.010 --> 00:53:35.810
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:53:35.810 --> 00:53:37.130
Thanks.

00:53:37.130 --> 00:53:41.640
In regards to your inputs,
what data are you discarding?

00:53:41.640 --> 00:53:44.600
Are you using everything
you've captured

00:53:44.600 --> 00:53:47.480
since the beginning, or
everything from the last patch,

00:53:47.480 --> 00:53:48.830
for the last two patches--

00:53:48.830 --> 00:53:50.660
or updates, rather?

00:53:50.660 --> 00:53:51.260
JUN: Yeah.

00:53:51.260 --> 00:53:54.400
Actually, we are using almost
